text:
The cycle of the book





flowchart LR
  A["Data"] --&gt; B["Explore"] --&gt; C["Stylized facts"] --&gt; D["Model"] --&gt; E["Compare with data"] --&gt; F["Revise"]
  F -- "a sharper question" --&gt; A
  F -- "a better idea" --&gt; D
  %% Colors live in the theme files (_includes/theme-*.scss), so the diagram follows the light/dark toggle
  classDef data stroke-width:1.5px;
  classDef model stroke-width:1.5px;
  class A,B,C data;
  class D,E,F model;






Every chapter runs through this cycle at least once. The blue steps are data science; the green steps are macroeconomic modeling. You start with the data and explore them until a pattern stands out. The pattern becomes a stylized fact. A model proposes an explanation, and you compare its predictions with the data. The comparison sends you back either to the data with a sharper question, or to the model with a better idea. In Appendix H the comparison becomes formal: the data estimate the model’s parameters, and the likelihood measures the fit.

describe the big facts behind the questions of macroeconomics, using real data for many countries;
explain the structure of an economic model: parameters and exogenous variables go in, and endogenous variables come out;
derive the equilibrium wage and employment of the supply-and-demand model of the labor market, read the comparative statics of each parameter, and derive the unemployment a minimum wage creates;
derive the compound-growth formula, the average annual growth rate and the rule of 70, and explain why steady growth draws a straight line on a ratio scale;
derive the link between the employment-population ratio and the unemployment rate, and measure the output gap;
tell the long run (economic growth) from the short run (fluctuations around potential output);
load data tables with pandas, check them, and draw interactive line charts with Plotly;
write short Python functions and turn them into interactive figures with sliders.

derive why production = expenditure = income: value added adds up to final sales, and the national income identity \(Y = C + I + G + NX\) splits GDP by who buys it;
describe how the composition of U.S. GDP and labor’s share of income have changed since 1929, and derive the labor-share measure that splits proprietors’ income;
derive the Laspeyres, Paasche and chain-weighted growth rates of real GDP as weighted averages, and split nominal growth into inflation and real growth;
compare GDP across countries with exchange rates and purchasing power parity, and read the comparative statics of each conversion;
read CSV files with pandas, compute new columns, work with dates, and draw area and line charts with Plotly Express;
compare countries, and check that two data sources use the same definitions before you compare them.

describe how recent sustained growth is, and how unevenly it spread across countries;
define a growth rate and derive the constant growth rule \(y_t = y_0(1+\bar{g})^t\) by applying one year’s growth again and again, and explain why compounding makes the yearly increase grow;
take logs to show why a ratio scale draws constant growth as a straight line, and derive the exact doubling time and the Rule of 70 from it;
derive the formula for the average annual growth rate between any two years, explain why it is a geometric mean, and compute it from data;
derive the rules for the growth rates of products, ratios and powers from logarithms, apply them to the Cobb-Douglas production function, and read their comparative statics;
reshape, filter, combine and summarize data tables with pandas, and draw line charts, scatter plots and step charts with Plotly Express;
go beyond the textbook’s countries: pick any of about 170 countries and compare data from two sources.

write down the Cobb-Douglas production function and derive, with the rules for exponents, its constant returns to scale and its diminishing marginal products;
derive the marginal products and the firm’s hiring rules from profit maximization, and solve the model’s five equations step by step for the equilibrium wage, rental price of capital, output and output per person;
show that labor gets two-thirds of output and capital one-third, so that firms make zero profit;
read the comparative statics of the equilibrium as elasticities: what more capital, more workers or higher productivity do to output, the wage and the rental price;
derive the development-accounting split of an income gap into a capital part and a TFP part, and compare the model’s predictions with data on 111 countries;
measure total factor productivity (TFP) as the part of income the model cannot explain with capital;
write Python functions with several arguments, combine Plotly Express charts with model curves, and select, filter and summarize rows of a large data table with pandas.

draw the Solow diagram and explain why an economy moves toward a steady state;
derive the model’s two key equations from its five assumptions, and solve them step by step for the steady-state levels of capital, output and consumption per person;
read the comparative statics of the steady state as elasticities, and explain why productivity matters more than investment;
derive how fast an economy grows on its way to the steady state, and how population growth changes the model;
run experiments: a higher investment rate, a higher depreciation rate, faster population growth, or an earthquake that destroys capital;
use the principle of transition dynamics to read cross-country data on growth and investment;
write short Python functions, draw interactive figures with Plotly, and work with data tables in pandas.

explain why ideas are nonrival, show with exponents that nonrivalry means increasing returns, and show with a fixed cost why prices must lie above marginal cost if anyone is to pay for research;
derive the Romer model’s balanced growth path step by step: the stock of ideas, the level of income per person and the long-run growth rate \(g_y^* = \gamma\bar{n}\), and the transition after a parameter change;
read the comparative statics as growth effects and level effects, including the hump-shaped effect of the research share, and run experiments with the Romer model and the combined Solow–Romer model;
derive the combined model’s growth rate \(\tfrac{3}{2}\gamma\bar{n}\), its capital-output ratio and its level, and derive the growth-accounting decomposition;
read long-run data on commodity prices, U.S. growth and growth accounting;
simulate a model year by year with a for loop, draw ratio-scale charts in Plotly, and compute growth rates over periods with pandas;
go beyond the textbook’s data: compare 40 commodities and 185 countries with a picker of your own.

read the main U.S. labor-market statistics: the employment-population ratio and the unemployment rate, and how both move in recessions;
link the unemployment rate, the participation rate and the employment-population ratio with one identity;
derive the equilibrium wage and employment of a supply-and-demand model of the labor market, read its comparative statics, and use it for experiments: an income tax, a fall in labor demand, and a wage that does not fall;
derive the natural rate of unemployment from the bathtub model, and the path that unemployment follows on its way there;
derive the present discounted value formulas from the geometric series, and use them to value a working life and a college degree;
explain with two labor markets why the college wage premium rose while the supply of college graduates grew;
load monthly data with pandas, shade recessions, recompute textbook tables, and build interactive Plotly figures with sliders, toggles, menus and buttons;
go beyond the textbook’s countries: compare up to 187 countries with a country picker, and spot breaks, gaps and implausible values in the data.

measure inflation from a price index, derive average inflation and the rule of 70 from the definition, and convert a price from one year into the dollars of another year with the consumer price index (CPI);
derive the quantity theory of money step by step from its four assumptions, and solve it for the price level, \(P^* = \bar{M}\bar{V}/\bar{Y}\), and for inflation, \(\pi^* = \bar{g}_M - \bar{g}_Y\);
read the comparative statics of the quantity theory, and explain why money is neutral in the long run (the classical dichotomy);
test the quantity theory with 150 years of U.S. data and with 165 economies;
derive the Fisher equation, \(i = R + \pi\), from the return on one saved dollar, and use it to compute real interest rates and to see who gains and who loses from surprise inflation;
derive how much revenue a government raises by printing money (seigniorage), and explain why very high inflation is usually a fiscal problem;
rebuild a price index from inflation rates, compute average growth rates over periods, use ratio scales with clear tick labels, and spot suspicious numbers in a data set.

split actual output into potential output \(\bar{Y}_t\) and short-run output \(\tilde{Y}_t\), derive its percent, log and growth-rate forms, and compute \(\tilde{Y}_t\) from data;
describe U.S. recessions with data on output, employment and inflation, and measure what they cost;
explain the Phillips curve and solve it forward in time, to derive the inflation path of a lasting boom and the output cost of bringing inflation down;
use Okun’s law to move between short-run output and unemployment, derive its growth-rate form, and read the comparative statics of both relationships (sign and slope of each effect, and which line moves);
in Python: shade recessions on a chart, fit a straight line with np.polyfit, compare two vintages of the same data, and simulate inflation year by year with a loop.

tell the story of the financial crisis with data: the housing bubble, the Fed’s interest rate, the spreads between interest rates and the fall of the stock market;
compare the Covid-19 recession across countries, in lives lost and in GDP lost;
measure how deep the two recessions were (employment, short-run output, unemployment, Table 10.1) and describe the inflation that followed the pandemic;
derive the measurement tools behind the figures: average growth rates, the climb back after a crash, short-run output, the cumulative GDP loss, and the gap between log changes and percent changes;
use a balance sheet to derive how leverage magnifies returns, how small a fall in asset prices wipes out net worth, and why losses force asset sales; solve a simple model of a bank run;
read an interest rate spread as lenders’ fear of default, split a fall in GDP into the contributions of its components, and read the comparative statics of each result;
in Python: find peaks and troughs with idxmax and idxmin, shade recessions with one small function, reshape a table with melt, compare two vintages of the same data, and turn log changes into percent changes;
go beyond the textbook’s countries: pick any of up to 61 economies from long (tidy) data files, and spot breaks, gaps and changes of definition in international data.

derive the IS curve \(\tilde{Y}_t = \bar{a} - \bar{b}(R_t - \bar{r})\) step by step from the national income identity and the five demand equations, and explain why it slopes down and why “IS” means investment equals saving;
read the comparative statics of the IS curve: tell a movement along it (a change in the real interest rate) from a shift (an aggregate demand shock or a change in the marginal product of capital), and say by how much output changes;
derive the multiplier \(1/(1-\bar{x})\) in two ways, by solving the model and by adding up the rounds of spending;
explain the life-cycle / permanent-income theory of consumption, compute permanent income as a present value, and explain why only the present value of taxes matters (Ricardian equivalence);
compare the Congressional Budget Office’s 2009 forecasts of unemployment with what actually happened;
in Python: reuse one model function in many figures, shade areas between two curves, and summarize monthly data by quarter and year with groupby.

derive the MP curve from the Fisher equation and sticky inflation, and explain why a central bank that sets a nominal interest rate also sets the real rate in the short run;
use the IS/MP diagram to analyze monetary policy and demand shocks, and derive the interest rate that keeps output at potential;
derive the Phillips curve \(\Delta\pi_t = \bar{\nu}\tilde{Y}_t + \bar{o}\) from price setting with adaptive expectations and price shocks, and read its comparative statics;
combine the IS, MP and Phillips curves into one equation for inflation, solve it for a disinflation and its cost in lost output, simulate the Volcker disinflation year by year, explain how mistakes contributed to the Great Inflation, and preview how an interest-rate rule (the Taylor rule) brings inflation back to target;
read U.S. data on the federal funds rate, the yield curve, inflation and the Covid-19 recession, and compare them with dozens of other countries;
in Python: draw several panels with make_subplots, simulate a model with a loop, reshape tables with melt and pivot, join tables with merge, animate a chart with animation_frame, and fit a trend with np.polyfit.

derive the aggregate demand (AD) curve by substituting a monetary policy rule into the IS curve, and read the Phillips curve as an aggregate supply (AS) curve;
derive the model’s steady state, its solution for one period, and the rule by which the inflation gap shrinks each period; use them to follow an inflation shock, a disinflation and an aggregate demand shock;
read the comparative statics: how the policy response, the slope of the AS curve and the shocks change the first-period effects, the speed of recovery and the total output cost; explain why expectations, credibility and inflation targets matter for the cost of fighting inflation;
test the model on U.S. data, and on data for dozens of other countries: policy rates against the policy rule, inflation-output loops, and three centuries of inflation;
in Python: write one simulate loop and reuse it in many figures, build tables from lists of dictionaries, reshape data with melt, join tables with merge, and compare two vintages of the same data.

add the financial friction \(\bar{f}\) to the IS/MP model, derive the new AD curve step by step, and show why the friction acts like a negative demand shock;
solve the AS/AD model period by period for output and inflation, derive the long run and the speed of adjustment, and read the comparative statics of a higher risk premium;
derive the best output the Fed can reach at the zero lower bound, and show with the Fisher equation why a deflationary spiral can turn a recession into a depression;
use balance-sheet algebra (leverage, capital requirements), present values and the term structure to explain the policy responses: bank rescues, quantitative easing, forward guidance and fiscal stimulus;
read the data on the crisis and its aftermath (interest-rate spreads, the Taylor rule, potential GDP, money growth, asset-price bubbles, the Fed’s balance sheet, forward guidance and productivity), and compare the U.S. experience with dozens of other economies;
in Python: build a model step by step from small functions, compute peaks, troughs and growth rates, stack a chart with px.area, rebuild a table from monthly data, blank out data that cross a definition break, and pick countries from a list (SelectMultiple) with a color that stays fixed for each country.

explain the letters D, S and GE, the building blocks of a DSGE model (agents, shocks, frictions), and how real business cycle models grew into DSGE models;
derive the impulse response and the half-life of a persistent shock, and derive TFP growth as a Solow residual from the production function, including how changes in utilization bias it;
read data on total factor productivity (TFP) growth, for the United States and 120 other countries, and see why the way TFP is measured changes the answer to “do TFP shocks cause recessions?”;
derive labor supply \(L^s = \bar{\ell}\,w/c\) from a household’s utility, solve the labor market for employment and the real wage, read its comparative statics as elasticities, and use it to study a TFP shock, a sales tax, government purchases and good news;
show how sticky wages and sticky prices change the effects of monetary policy on employment and the real wage;
say what an impulse response function shows and what the estimated Smets–Wouters model reports;
in Python: write one equilibrium function and reuse it in many figures, reshape data with melt, draw a scatter in two panels with a regression line, and use period sliders and toggle buttons.

derive the intertemporal budget constraint from the budgets of the two periods, and split total wealth into financial wealth and human wealth;
explain diminishing marginal utility, and why it makes people want to smooth consumption;
derive the Euler equation in two ways, with a small-move argument and with calculus, and solve the two-period neoclassical consumption model with log utility for any degree of patience \(\beta\);
compute the real interest rate that a given growth rate of consumption requires, and explain why interest rates and growth rates are numbers of similar size;
read the comparative statics of consumption: the marginal propensities to consume, the wealth effect of the interest rate, and the income and substitution effects;
extend the model to many periods and read it as the permanent-income and life-cycle hypotheses;
compute the marginal propensity to consume with and without borrowing constraints, see why Ricardian equivalence can fail, and explain the random walk of consumption and precautionary saving;
read data on household debt, the personal saving rate and household wealth, for the U.S. and dozens of other countries;
in Python: write functions with text options, find a maximum on a grid with np.argmax, turn monthly data into quarterly data with resample, join two tables with merge, and fit a line through a scatter.

use an arbitrage equation to compare two ways of investing the same money, and derive from it the user cost of capital, with and without a corporate income tax;
derive the desired capital stock, where the marginal product of capital equals the user cost, and the investment rate it implies, step by step from a Cobb-Douglas production function;
read the comparative statics of desired capital and the investment rate as elasticities, and rebuild Table 17.1 of corporate taxes and user costs for any year;
derive the price of a stock as the present value of its dividends, \(\text{dividend}/(R-g)\), and use it to read the price-earnings ratio of the stock market, Tobin’s \(q\) and the price of a house;
describe the components of private investment, in the U.S. and in 34 other countries, and how they move in recessions;
write Python functions with default values, draw Plotly figures with sliders and buttons, reshape tables with rename, melt and merge, and repeat steps with for and while loops.

read the U.S. federal budget: where the money goes, where it comes from, and how large the deficit is, in percent of GDP and in dollars per person (Table 18.1);
derive the flow budget constraint from “uses equal sources”, and use it to compute next year’s debt and the primary and total deficits;
derive the intertemporal budget constraint by substitution, for two and for more periods, and read its comparative statics: how much a tax cut today raises the taxes of the future;
derive the law of motion of the debt-GDP ratio with the growth rules, solve for the primary surplus that holds it constant and for its steady state, and state the sustainability conditions (\(i\) below or above \(g\));
explain why the debt-GDP ratio falls when GDP grows faster than the interest rate, and split the changes in the U.S. ratio into deficits and growth;
derive the saving-investment identity and use it to measure crowding out;
compare government spending and debt across countries, and describe the fiscal problem of the twenty-first century: the rise of health spending;
in Python: write small accounting functions, reshape tables with melt and merge, draw a chart with two vertical axes, animate a bar chart, and compare two vintages of the same data.

read the U.S. trade data: exports, imports and the trade balance (net exports) as shares of GDP;
derive \(NX = S - I\) step by step from the national income identity \(Y = C + I + G + NX\), explain why a trade deficit is borrowing from the rest of the world, and derive the country’s intertemporal budget constraint and the long-run foreign debt ratio from the way deficits add up to a foreign debt;
solve the textbook’s trade examples with algebra (exchange, trade across time, comparative advantage, free migration), derive the free-trade world price and the condition for specialization, read the comparative statics as elasticities, and say who gains from each arrangement;
compare trade balances across countries and periods, and check which year or which window a published number really refers to;
go beyond the textbook’s countries: pick any economy from World Bank, Penn World Table, IMF and External Wealth of Nations data, and check one source against another;
in Python: write small accounting functions once and reuse them, reshape tables with melt and pivot, draw grouped bar charts, average over a window with .loc[start:end], and add up flows with cumsum.

tell the nominal exchange rate (the price of a currency) from the real exchange rate (the price of goods), and derive the long-run exchange rate from the law of one price and the quantity theory, in levels and in growth rates (purchasing power parity), and the drift of real exchange rates (Balassa-Samuelson);
derive uncovered interest parity and use it to explain why the exchange rate jumps with interest rates and why a fixed exchange rate ties a country’s interest rate to the rest of the world;
test the law of one price on data: the yen and the dollar since 1971, and the Big Mac index;
derive the open-economy IS curve and the AD curve, read their comparative statics (a flatter IS curve, a rise in foreign interest rates as an aggregate demand shock), and solve the AS/AD dynamics that follow;
explain the policy trilemma and read the history of exchange-rate regimes, currency crises and the euro crisis in the data;
in Python: build price levels from inflation rates with cumprod, plot two vertical scales in one figure, switch views with Plotly buttons, rebase an index, and reshape tables with melt;
go beyond the textbook’s countries: pick any of up to 200 economies from a list (SelectMultiple), filter rows with isin, compare data from the World Bank, the IMF, the OECD and the Penn World Table, and spot gaps, breaks and differences in definitions.

state the five main lessons of the book in your own words: living standards, transition dynamics, the classical dichotomy, the short-run Phillips curve trade-off and central-bank credibility;
restate the key results of every model in the book and derive them step by step from their building blocks: development accounting, the Solow steady state, long-run growth from ideas, the natural rate of unemployment, the quantity theory, the IS, Phillips and AD curves, the path of inflation in a disinflation, the Euler equation, the user cost of capital and the government’s budget constraint;
read the comparative statics of each model: which parameter moves which result, in which direction and by how much, and check the headline results with short Python simulations;
find the model and the figure in earlier notebooks where each lesson was built;
recompute a key number for each lesson from real data, such as Japan’s catch-up growth or the slope of the U.S. Phillips curve, and compare Japan’s catch-up with any of 167 other economies in a country picker;
name a few open questions at the frontier of research, such as the productivity slowdown after 2003;
reuse the Python you have learned: read_csv, filtering rows, merge, pivot, short functions and np.polyfit.

explain what Google Colab is, why this book uses it, and what its limits are;
open any notebook of the book in Colab, run it, move its sliders, save your own copy and close it properly;
write short Python programs with numbers, variables, lists, loops, if statements and functions, using examples from the book’s models;
use Gemini in Colab to explain code, write new code, fix errors, run a whole analysis and tutor you step by step, and check what it produces before you trust it;
load, explore and reshape a real data table with pandas, compute with NumPy, draw interactive charts with Plotly, and add sliders with ipywidgets;
put these pieces together in a small data project, first by hand and then with AI: which economies grew fastest after 1990?

name the main sources of macroeconomic data and say what each one is good for;
tell apart the four ways of getting data into Python: a specialized library, a general library for many agencies, a collection of many sources in one place, and reading a file straight from its web address;
download series from FRED, the World Bank, the IMF, the ECB, Eurostat and the OECD with a few lines of code;
read the Penn World Table, the Maddison Project Database and Our World in Data directly from their files on the internet;
compare the same number from several sources and explain why they differ;
keep good habits: find the right series code, check units and dates, save a copy and record your sources.

work with percentages, ratios and index numbers, and tell a percent change from a change in percentage points;
use exponents and logarithms to describe compound growth, read a ratio scale and apply the Rule of 70;
compute growth rates three ways, apply the growth rules for products, ratios and powers, and know when an approximation such as \(i \approx R + \pi\) is good enough;
use derivatives for marginal products, elasticities and optimization, and solve equations and systems of equations;
simulate difference equations (how capital, unemployment, inflation and public debt move over time), value future payments with present value, and separate the trend of a series from its cycle;
do all of this in Python, with NumPy for numbers, SymPy for algebra and calculus with letters, and SciPy for numerical solvers.

explain what the Penn World Table is, find the variables that the two methods need, and load them in Python, both from the official file and from the book’s prepared file;
derive the growth accounting equation from a Cobb–Douglas production function, and compute the contributions of capital, labour and TFP to GDP growth for every year since 1990;
derive the development accounting equation in its capital–output form, and split each economy’s income gap with the United States into TFP, physical capital and human capital;
measure how much of the differences in income across countries the inputs can explain, with the “success” measure of development accounting;
test how the answers change with the capital share, the measure of labour, country labour shares and natural-resource rents;
write short pandas functions (groupby, pct_change, merge, melt) and draw interactive Plotly charts that update when you choose other countries.

derive the speed of convergence \(\beta=(1-\alpha)(\bar{n}+\bar{g}+\bar{d})\) and the half-life \(\ln 2/\beta\) from the Solow model, and turn the model into a regression of growth on initial income;
tell β-convergence (poor countries grow faster) from σ-convergence (incomes become more equal), and explain why the first does not guarantee the second;
estimate β-convergence by OLS with robust standard errors, convert the slope into a speed and a half-life, and estimate the speed directly by nonlinear least squares (NLS);
check whether the answer depends on the period, with a structural break, rolling windows and a heatmap of every start and end year;
measure σ-convergence with the variance of log income and its confidence interval;
do all of this in Python with pandas, statsmodels, SciPy and interactive Plotly charts.

derive the steady-state income equation of the textbook Solow model and turn it into a regression of income on the investment rate and population growth;
derive the steady state of the augmented Solow model, with physical and human capital, in its two forms: with the investment rate in human capital, and with its level;
estimate both models with OLS in statsmodels, test the restrictions that the theory puts on the coefficients, and recover the capital shares that the estimates imply;
explain conditional convergence, derive the speed of convergence of the augmented model, and estimate it from growth regressions;
use interactive simulations to see why human capital makes saving matter more and slows convergence;
rebuild MRW’s variables from the Penn World Table 11.0 and the World Bank, and check whether their results survive with new data, new countries and a new period.

explain what makes a model dynamic, stochastic and general equilibrium, and what it means to solve and to estimate one;
solve a model with rational expectations by hand, and say why it needs the Blanchard–Kahn condition;
read and write a Snowdrop model file, and simulate three models with it: the AS/AD model of Chapter 13, the monetary policy model of the Snowdrop paper, and a real business cycle model;
read impulse responses and contribution charts, and check that a numerical solution is right;
explain how the Kalman filter turns a model into a likelihood, and estimate a parameter by maximum likelihood with a few lines of NumPy;
estimate Ireland’s (2004) New Keynesian model on U.S. data by maximum likelihood and by Bayesian posterior mode, and judge how much the priors matter.

read the 14 equations of the Smets–Wouters model, say what each one does, and link it to the simple models of Chapter 15;
read a research model file with care: find what it changes, what it gets wrong and what it leaves out;
solve the model with Snowdrop, check that the solution is right, and compute impulse responses in the units that the textbook uses;
recompute the five Smets–Wouters figures of Chapter 15 (a monetary policy shock, a TFP shock, a rise in government purchases and a financial friction shock), and compare the numbers with those that Jones (2024) reports;
run experiments: switch frictions off one at a time and find out which of them produce the patterns of Chapter 15.

I.2 From Chapter 15 to an estimated model
Chapter 15 built a DSGE model in small pieces: TFP shocks in the Solow model, a labor market in which labor demand and labor supply set employment and the real wage, and sticky wages or sticky prices that let monetary policy move output. Each piece answered one question at a time, with “consumption taken as given”.
Smets and Wouters (2007) put all of these pieces, and more, into one model, and estimated it on U.S. data from 1966 to 2004. Their model builds on Christiano, Eichenbaum and Evans (2005). It has:

households that like consumption, with habits (they care about consumption relative to last quarter’s), and dislike work;
firms that rent capital, decide how intensively to use it (capacity utilization), and pay a cost when they change investment quickly (investment adjustment costs);
sticky prices and sticky wages: each quarter only some firms reset their prices and only some households reset their wages (the Calvo model), and the others index them to past inflation;
a central bank that sets the interest rate with a Taylor rule (Chapter 12);
seven shocks: to TFP, to a risk premium (the financial friction of textbook Fig. 15.13), to government purchases, to the efficiency of investment, to monetary policy, and to price and wage markups.

“Estimated” means that the parameters were chosen so that the model fits seven U.S. series (the growth of GDP, consumption, investment and the real wage, hours worked, inflation and the federal funds rate), with the Bayesian method that Appendix H.10–H.12 introduced on a smaller model. We do not re-estimate the model here. We take Smets and Wouters’ estimates, the posterior mode of their Table 1, and study what the model says.
The tool for that is the impulse response function of Chapter 15: the path of a variable, quarter by quarter, after one shock hits the economy, compared with the path without the shock. The model has 7 shocks and dozens of variables, so it has hundreds of impulse responses; Chapter 15 shows 17 of them.
